( 12... Ne4?
{13.Nxe4 and nothing takes back: the bishop went to h5 two moves ago and the knight on d7 does not reach e4.}
) ( 12... Ne8?!
{Passive. On d5 the knight does a job straight away; on e8 it does nothing for several moves.}
) 13. Nxd5 {White trades before the knight settles in for good. [%weight 7]} 13... cxd5
{Take towards the centre. The pawn on d5 is solid and the c-file opens for a rook. [%tags c-file] [%plan Play ...Be7 to keep the bishop, bring a rook to c8, and hit the pawn on e5 with ...f6 once the pieces are ready.] [%line The full main line, with 6.e3]}
( 13... exd5?!
{It leaves a backward pawn on c6 and closes the file Black wants. Take with the c-pawn.} )
*


[CourseId "slav-black"]
[CourseTitle "The Slav Defence"]
[StudentColour "black"]
[Tagline "One structure against 1.d4, with the bishop outside, as Black."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "vs-quiet vs-exchange-slav vs-catalan vs-london light-squares c5-break e5-break c-file"]
[ChapterId "ch2-quiet"]
[ChapterTitle "The quiet build-up: 4.e3 and 4.Bf4"]
[DefaultTags "vs-quiet"]

{White develops without touching the tension in the centre. These are the easiest positions in the course to play, and the two moves are not as alike as they look: against 4.e3 the bishop comes straight out, and against 4.Bf4 it should wait.

## Against 4.e3, the bishop goes out at once

4.e3 shuts White's own bishop on c1 in behind the pawn chain. That is White's business, and it means White has no quick way to punish 4...Bf5, so Black takes the free move. Everything after that is ordinary development: ...e6, ...Nbd7, a bishop to d6, castle.

## Against 4.Bf4, take the pawn instead

This is the one line in the course where ...Bf5 is not the move. With a bishop already on f4 supporting the advance, 4...Bf5 5.Qb3 Qb6 6.c5! clamps the queenside and Black has no comfortable break. Play 4...dxc4 instead, which is exactly what chapter 1 does, and bring the bishop out a move or two later once White has spent time recovering the pawn.

## The trick to know after 4.e3

5.cxd5 cxd5 6.Qb3 hits b7, which the bishop no longer guards, and d5 at the same time. The answer is 6...Qc7. It defends b7, and it puts the queen on the open file opposite a bishop that has never moved, so 7.Qxd5 loses a piece to 7...Qxc1+.

## The knight trip to h4, and what the h-file is for

After 5.Nc3 e6 White often plays 6.Nh4 to trade the bishop off. Let it happen, and recapture on g6 with the h-pawn. The rook on h8 then sits on an open file, and that is not decoration: if White ever castles short and plays a natural move like Qc2, ...Bxh2+ wins on the spot, because the king cannot take a bishop the rook is defending.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Playing ...e6 first out of habit against 4.e3, when nothing is stopping ...Bf5. Answering Qb3 with ...b6 or a retreat, when ...Qc7 solves everything and sets a trap. Recapturing on g6 with the f-pawn, which leaves a hole on e6 and gives up the file. And playing ...Bf5 against 4.Bf4 because it is the move everywhere else.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. e3
{The Slow Slav. White builds a small, safe position and shuts in the bishop on c1 to do it. [%weight 7]}
( 4. Bf4
{White gets the bishop out before e3. It is more ambitious than the Slow Slav, and it changes what Black should do. [%weight 4]}
4... dxc4
{Take, exactly as in chapter 1. With a bishop already on f4 the immediate ...Bf5 runs into Qb3 and c5, so the pawn is the better target. [%tags development light-squares]}
( 4... Bf5?!
{The natural move, and this is the one line where it is not the best: 5.Qb3 Qb6 6.c5! clamps the queenside and Black has no comfortable break.}
) ( 4... e6!?
{Solid, and it shuts Black's bishop in while White's is already outside. That is the wrong way round.}
) 5. e3 {White props up d4 and prepares to recover the pawn with the bishop. [%weight 6]}
( 5. a4
{White stops ...b5 first, exactly as in the main line, and worries about the pawn afterwards. [%weight 6]}
5... e6
{Make room for the bishop. The pawn on c4 cannot be held once a4 is in, so develop instead of clinging to it. [%tags development]}
( 5... b5!?
{Holding the pawn works here too, because there is no knight on c3 to make b3 bite. Giving it back and developing is simpler.}
) 6. Nc3 {White develops and prepares to take the pawn back with the bishop. [%weight 6]}
6... Nd5
{Hit the bishop on f4 and blockade in the middle. A knight on d5 is worth more than the pawn on c4. [%tags bishop-placement]}
( 6... Bb4!?
{Also natural. Hitting the bishop first asks a question White has to answer before recovering the pawn.}
) 7. Bg5
{The bishop keeps the diagonal and takes aim at the squares behind the knight. [%weight 6]}
7... Be7
{Offer the trade. With the dark-squared bishops off, the knight on d5 has nothing left to fear. [%tags development] [%plan Trade on g5 or e7, castle, then play ...b5 to hold the extra pawn or ...Nd7 and ...c5 to give it back for the centre.] [%line 4.Bf4 and the 5.a4 clamp]}
( 7... f6!?
{It kicks the bishop and weakens e6 with the king still in the middle. Developing does the same job safely.}
) ) ( 5. Nc3
{White develops the knight first and keeps every recapture available. [%weight 5]} 5... e6
{Open the path for the bishop on f8 and get ready to give the pawn back on Black's terms. [%tags development]}
6. a4
{White stops ...b5 before taking the pawn, and the position is now the 5.a4 line above by another order. [%weight 6] [%plan Play ...Nd5, hitting the bishop on f4, then ...Be7 and castle. This is the same position the 5.a4 line reaches a move earlier.] [%line 4.Bf4 and 5.Nc3]}
) 5... b5
{Hold the pawn, as in chapter 1's 5.e3 line. Winning it back is going to cost White real time. [%tags development]}
( 5... e6?!
{It hands the pawn straight back after 6.Bxc4. Holding it costs White real time and Black risks nothing by doing so.}
) 6. Be2
{White develops and prepares a4, which is the only way to get at the pawn chain. [%weight 6]}
6... e6
{Open the path for the dark-squared bishop. The other one comes out to b7 later, along the diagonal ...b5 opened. [%tags development]}
7. O-O {White castles and leaves the pawn for now. [%weight 7]} 7... Be7
{Develop and get ready to castle. Black is a pawn up and every piece has a square. [%tags development king-safety] [%plan Castle, play ...a5 to hold the chain and ...Bb7 on the long diagonal, and give the pawn on c4 back for the open file when White finally attacks it.] [%line The 4.Bf4 build-up]}
( 7... a5!?
{The right idea in the wrong order. Get the king out of the middle before fixing the queenside.}
) ) 4... Bf5
{Out it goes, for free. White has no quick way to punish it, because the bishop on c1 is behind its own pawn. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares]}
( 4... e6!?
{Solid, and it is the very move this repertoire is arranged to avoid. There is no reason to shut Black's bishop in as well.}
) ( 4... Bg4!?
{Also good, and it pins the knight. From f5 the bishop watches e4, which is the square White eventually wants.}
) ( 4... dxc4!?
{Fine too, and 5.Bxc4 gives White the free developing move that a4 lines make White work for.}
) 5. Nc3
{Normal development, and the knight defends c4 so that the tension can stay a while longer. [%weight 6]}
( 5. Bd3
{White offers the trade at once, hoping that the recapture improves the queen. [%weight 5]}
5... Bxd3
{Take. Declining costs a move and the bishop has no better square than the one it would go to anyway. [%tags bishop-placement]}
( 5... Bg6!?
{Keeping the bishop is reasonable, and 6.Bxg6 hxg6 happens next move on White's terms rather than Black's.}
) ( 5... e6?!
{6.Bxf5 exf5 leaves Black with a pawn on f5 and no bishop, which is the wrong half of the trade.}
) 6. Qxd3
{The recapture puts the queen on an active square, which is what White was playing for. [%weight 9]}
6... e6
{Solid. With the light-squared bishop already off the board there is nothing left to shut in. [%tags development]}
7. O-O {White castles and thinks about the e4 break. [%weight 7]} 7... Nbd7
{Develop and cover e5 and c5, the two squares White would like a piece on. [%tags development]}
8. Nc3 {The last minor piece comes out and adds a third attacker to d5. [%weight 6]}
8... Be7
{Modest and correct. On d6 the bishop would run into e4 with tempo once the centre opens. [%tags development king-safety]}
( 8... Bd6!?
{Active, and 9.e4 dxe4 10.Nxe4 hits the bishop, so Black loses a move putting it somewhere else.}
) 9. e4
{The break White has been building towards. The centre opens while White has the freer pieces. [%weight 6]}
9... dxe4
{Take. Leaving the tension lets White play e5 and gain space for nothing. [%tags development]}
10. Nxe4 {White recaptures with a piece and keeps a small lead in space. [%weight 8]}
10... Nxe4
{Trade a pair off. Every trade helps the side with less space, which here is Black. [%tags development]}
11. Qxe4 {The queen takes back and sits in the middle of the board. [%weight 8]} 11... Nf6
{Hit the queen and develop at the same time, which is exactly the tempo Black needs. [%tags development] [%plan Castle, then play ...c5 to trade the last central pawn off. With the light-squared bishops gone, an equal endgame is a fine outcome.] [%line 4.e3 and the 5.Bd3 trade]}
( 11... O-O!?
{Safe, and it lets White keep the queen in the centre for another move. Gaining the tempo first is better.}
) ) ( 5. cxd5
{White releases the tension and follows with Qb3, which is the one real try against an early ...Bf5. [%weight 5]}
5... cxd5
{Take with the c-pawn. It keeps a pawn on d5 and opens the c-file for a rook. [%tags c-file]}
( 5... Nxd5?! {6.e4 hits the knight and takes the whole centre with tempo.} ) 6. Qb3
{The point. The queen hits b7, which the bishop stopped guarding, and d5 at the same time. [%weight 6]}
6... Qc7
{Defend b7 and take the open file. The bishop on c1 has never moved, so 7.Qxd5 loses a piece to 7...Qxc1+. [%tags tactics c-file]}
( 6... Qb6!?
{Offering the trade is solid, and 7.Qxb6 axb6 leaves Black with doubled pawns and White a shade more comfortable.}
) ( 6... b6?!
{It defends b7 with a pawn and leaves c6 and a6 to White's pieces. The queen defends the same pawn and develops.}
) ( 6... Bc8?!
{It holds the position and undoes two moves. There is no need to retreat when a developing move solves everything.}
) 7. Bb5+ {White develops with check before Black can finish the queenside. [%weight 5]} (
7. Bd2
{White steps the bishop out of the queen's line before doing anything else, which is the sensible answer. [%weight 6]}
7... a6
{Take b5 away from the bishop and the knight, and prepare ...Nc6 without a check landing in the middle of it. [%tags development]}
8. Nc3 {White develops and puts a third piece near d5. [%weight 6]} 8... e6
{Finish the pawn structure. Black is solid, with the better bishop and the open file. [%tags development] [%plan Play ...Nc6, ...Be7 and castle, then double on the c-file. The queen on c7 is already the first piece on it.] [%line 5.cxd5 with 7.Bd2]}
( 8... Nc6?!
{9.Nxd5! Nxd5 10.Qxd5 wins a pawn: with the queen on c7 rather than d8, the pawn on d5 has one defender against two attackers. ...e6 fixes that first.}
) ) ( 7. Nc3
{White develops and leaves the bishop on c1 where it is, which Black should notice. [%weight 5]}
7... e6
{Solid, and the trap stays set: the bishop on c1 is still facing a queen down an open file. [%tags c-file] [%plan Develop with ...Nc6, ...Be7 and ...O-O, then put a rook on c8. Keep watching c1: any queen move that leaves it undefended runs into a check on that square.] [%line 5.cxd5 with 7.Nc3]}
( 7... Qxc3+?
{The knight is defended by the pawn on b2, so this is simply a queen for a knight.} ) )
7... Nc6
{Block with the knight, which develops and puts a second defender on d5. [%tags development]}
( 7... Nbd7!?
{Also a block, and the knight has less to do on d7 than on c6 in this structure.} ) 8. O-O
{White castles and keeps the pin on the knight. [%weight 6]} 8... e6
{Solid, with the bishop long since outside the chain. [%tags development]} 9. Bd2
{White finally moves the bishop off the square Black's queen has been eyeing. [%weight 5]}
9... Be7
{Develop and prepare to castle. Black has no weaknesses and a rook heading for the c-file. [%tags development king-safety] [%plan Castle, then play ...a6 to question the bishop on b5 and put a rook on c8. The pawn on d5 is solid and the file is Black's to use.] [%line 4.e3 and the 5.cxd5 trade]}
( 9... a6!?
{Also useful, and it invites 10.Bxc6+ bxc6, which hands White a target on c6. Castle first.}
) ) 5... e6
{Now the pawn goes to e6, behind the bishop rather than in front of it. [%tags development]}
( 5... Nbd7!?
{Also fine and it usually transposes. ...e6 keeps the dark-squared bishop's options open.}
) ( 5... dxc4?!
{6.Bxc4 gives White the centre and a developing move at once, and Black has no ...b5 to gain time with.}
) 6. Nh4
{White spends two moves to trade the bishop, which is the standard plan against ...Bf5 in every line of this opening. [%weight 5]}
6... Bg6
{Step back one square and let the trade happen. The bishop has already done its job by making White spend the moves. [%tags bishop-placement]}
( 6... Be4?!
{7.f3 asks the bishop to move again, and it goes back to g6 anyway a move down.} ) (
6... Bg4?!
{7.f3 sends the bishop back to a worse square than g6, and it has achieved nothing on the way.}
) 7. Nxg6
{White carries out the plan, spending two knight moves and giving up a knight for the bishop. [%weight 8]}
7... hxg6
{Take with the h-pawn. The rook on h8 gets an open file without moving, and the pawns stay healthy. [%tags h-file]}
( 7... fxg6?
{It leaves a hole on e6, blocks the bishop on f8 from ever seeing the kingside, and gives up the h-file for nothing.}
) 8. Bd3
{White develops and eyes the diagonal that the bishop trade opened up. [%weight 6]}
8... Nbd7
{Develop towards the centre. The knight covers e5 and clears the back rank. [%tags development]}
( 8... Bd6!?
{Also natural, and it usually transposes. Knights before bishops is the safer habit here.}
) 9. O-O {White castles and prepares to break with e4 at some point. [%weight 7]} 9... Bd6
{The bishop takes the best diagonal it has, aimed straight at h2 behind White's king. [%tags bishop-placement]}
( 9... Be7!?
{Solid and duller. On d6 the bishop holds e5 and points at h2, which is where this line can be decided.}
) 10. h3
{A small move and a necessary one. It takes g4 from the knight and gives the king an escape square. [%weight 6]}
( 10. Qc2
{A natural developing move, and here it is the losing one: nothing is watching h2 and the king has no escape square. [%weight 3]}
10... Bxh2+
{The bishop cannot be taken. The rook on h8 defends h2 down the file that ...hxg6 opened, so 11.Kxh2 is not even legal. [%tags tactics h-file]}
( 10... Qe7?
{It passes up a winning move. With a rook already on the open file behind it, the bishop sacrifice on h2 is worth checking every single move.}
) 11. Kh1
{Forced. The other three squares beside the king hold White's own pieces, and taking the bishop is not a legal move while the rook on h8 defends it. [%weight 9]}
11... Bd6+
{Step away with discovered check from the rook. Black is a pawn up, the attack is still running and White's king has nowhere safe to go. [%tags h-file tactics] [%plan Bring the knight to g4 and the queen to h4 or h5. Keep the bishop on the diagonal that ends on h2 and do not hurry: White cannot untangle.] [%line 4.e3 and the h2 pawn]}
) 10... Qe7
{Connect the rooks and keep both castling options open while White commits first. [%tags development] [%plan Castle long and use the open h-file, or castle short and prepare ...e5. The doubled g-pawns are not a weakness while the centre stays closed.] [%line The 4.e3 build-up, with 10.h3]}
( 10... O-O!?
{Fine, and it commits the king to the side where Black's own file attack would have been.}
) *


[CourseId "slav-black"]
[CourseTitle "The Slav Defence"]
[StudentColour "black"]
[Tagline "One structure against 1.d4, with the bishop outside, as Black."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "vs-quiet vs-exchange-slav vs-catalan vs-london light-squares c5-break e5-break c-file"]
[ChapterId "ch3-exchange"]
[ChapterTitle "The Exchange Slav: 3.cxd5"]
[DefaultTags "vs-exchange-slav"]

{White trades on d5 and the pawns are symmetrical. Most books shrug at this point and say the position is equal. It is, and there is still a great deal to do, because symmetrical positions are decided by who uses the one open file first.

## Recapture with the c-pawn

3...cxd5 keeps a pawn on d5, opens the c-file and leaves the bishop on c8 free to come out. Taking with the queen instead invites 4.Nc3, which develops a piece and gains a move on her.

## What Black is actually playing for

Three things, in order. The c-file: a rook on c8 is worth more than a rook on a random square, and Black gets to put one there first because White has spent moves on Bf4 and Bd3. The c4 square: a knight that reaches c4 through a5 cannot be driven off by a pawn without White playing b3, which loosens c3. And the b2 pawn: once the dark-squared bishops come off, ...Qb6 is a real threat rather than a gesture.

None of that wins a game by itself. What it does is give Black something to aim at while a club opponent shuffles, and shuffling is what most club opponents do in a symmetrical position.

## The one sequence to memorise

After 4.Nc3 Nf6 5.Bf4 Nc6 6.e3 Bf5 White often plays 7.Qb3, hitting b7 and d5 at once. The answer is 7...Na5. It looks like it puts the knight offside and it is the only move that solves everything: it defends b7 by attacking b7, so 8.Qxb7 loses the queen to 8...Nxb7. White checks on a4 instead, and the block has to be 8...Bd7, because the bishop attacks the queen back. Any other block and 9.Qxa5 takes the knight.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Recapturing on d5 with the queen and losing a move to Nc3. Meeting Qb3 with ...Rb8, which drops the exchange to Bxb8, or blocking the check on a4 with the queen, which drops the knight on a5. Playing ...e6 before the bishop is out, which is the same error as everywhere else in this course. And treating a symmetrical position as an agreed draw, then drifting until the one open file belongs to White.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. cxd5 cxd5
{Recapture with the c-pawn. It keeps a pawn on d5, opens the c-file and leaves the bishop on c8 free. [%tags c-file]}
( 3... Qxd5?
{4.Nc3 develops a piece and hits the queen, so White gets a free move. This is a Scandinavian, and a poor version of one.}
) ( 3... Nf6?!
{4.dxc6 Nxc6 leaves Black a pawn down for a lead in development. That is a gambit, and there is no reason to play one here.}
) 4. Nc3
{The main move. White develops and puts a second attacker on d5 before anything else. [%weight 6]}
( 4. Bf4
{The bishop comes out first, which is the move order a London player reaches for. [%weight 3]}
4... Nf6
{Develop and defend d5. The move order does not change what Black is doing. [%tags development]}
( 4... Nc6!?
{Also fine and it transposes. The kingside knight is the one that has to come out anyway.}
) 5. Nc3
{White develops the knight and the position is back in the main line. [%weight 7]}
5... Nc6
{Develop and hit d4. Black is a move away from the main line of the chapter. [%tags development] [%plan Answer 6.e3 with ...Bf5, then ...e6, ...Bd6 and castle. This is the same chapter by another road.] [%line 4.Bf4 first]}
( 5... e6?!
{It shuts the bishop in one move before it was going to come out. There is no hurry and no threat.}
) ) ( 4. Nf3
{A quiet move order that leads to the same positions as everything else in this chapter. [%weight 4]}
4... Nf6
{Develop and defend d5. Black plays the same five moves whatever White tries here. [%tags development]}
5. Nc3 {White develops and the game heads back to the main line. [%weight 7]} 5... Nc6
{The last minor piece with an obvious square. Black is now a move from the chapter's main position. [%tags development] [%plan Meet Bf4 with ...Bf5 and e3 with ...e6, then ...Bd6, castle and take the c-file.] [%line 4.Nf3 first]}
( 5... Bf5!?
{Also good, and 6.Qb3 comes with more bite before the knight reaches c6, because ...Na5 is not available yet.}
) ) 4... Nf6
{Develop and defend d5 a second time. Nothing fancy is needed in a symmetrical position. [%tags development]}
( 4... Nc6!?
{Also fine and it usually transposes. The knight on f6 is the one that has to come out before White gets ideas about d5.}
) ( 4... e6?!
{It shuts in the bishop on c8 in a position where nothing is stopping it from coming out.}
) 5. Bf4
{White gets the bishop outside the pawn chain, which is what Black is about to do as well. [%weight 6]}
( 5. Nf3
{White develops the other knight first, which usually transposes back into the same positions. [%weight 5]}
5... Nc6
{Develop and press d4. Black's set-up is the same whatever order White chooses. [%tags development]}
6. Bf4 {White puts the bishop outside the pawn chain, as usual in this line. [%weight 6]}
6... Bf5
{Out it goes. The bishops mirror each other and neither side can claim anything from it. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares] [%plan Play ...e6, then ...Bd6 or ...Be7, castle, and take the c-file. If Qb3 arrives, ...Na5 is still the answer.] [%line 5.Nf3 before the bishop]}
( 6... e6!? {Solid and unnecessary. The bishop has a free square and this takes it away.}
) ) 5... Nc6
{Develop towards the centre and add a second attacker to d4. [%tags development]} (
5... a6!?
{A modern touch, taking b5 away from White's pieces before developing. It costs a move Black does not have to spend.}
) ( 5... e6?!
{The bishop on c8 has a free path to f5 and this closes it. Develop the queenside knight first.}
) 6. e3 {White props up d4 and opens a line for the king's bishop. [%weight 6]} 6... Bf5
{Out before ...e6, exactly as in every other chapter. From f5 the bishop covers the squares White wants for a knight. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares]}
( 6... e6!?
{Solid, and it buries the bishop for no reason at all. Nothing is attacking it on f5.} ) (
6... Bg4!?
{Playable, and there is no knight on f3 yet to pin. On f5 the bishop is doing something straight away.}
) 7. Bd3
{White offers the trade of the good bishops, which suits a player content with a draw. [%weight 5]}
( 7. Qb3
{The critical try. The queen hits b7, which nothing is guarding, and d5 at the same time. [%weight 5]}
7... Na5
{The move to remember. It defends b7 by attacking it, so 8.Qxb7 loses the queen to 8...Nxb7, and the check on a4 is answered by a bishop that hits the queen back. [%tags tactics]}
( 7... Qd7?!
{It defends b7 and ties the queen to the job. ...Na5 defends the same pawn and gains a move as well.}
) ( 7... Rb8?
{8.Bxb8 wins the exchange. The bishop on f4 has been looking down the diagonal at b8 since move five.}
) ( 7... e6? {It defends d5 and forgets b7: 8.Qxb7 simply wins a pawn.} ) 8. Qa4+
{The queen has to leave, and she leaves with check so that Black cannot claim the whole tempo. [%weight 8]}
8... Bd7
{Block with the bishop, and notice that it attacks the queen on a4 as it does so. That is what saves the knight: 9.Qxa5 now loses the queen to 9...Bxa4. [%tags development]}
( 8... Qd7?
{9.Qxa5 simply takes the knight. Only the bishop can block, because only the bishop attacks the queen on a4 while it does.}
) ( 8... Nc6?!
{It undoes the last move and offers White a repetition where only White has a choice.} )
9. Qc2
{The queen goes back to a sensible square, two moves down on where she started. [%weight 7]}
9... e6
{Solid. The bishop has already been outside once, so this costs nothing. [%tags development]}
10. Nf3 {White develops the last knight and prepares to castle. [%weight 7]} 10... Rc8
{Take the open file before castling. The rook faces White's queen, which is a nuisance White has to answer. [%tags c-file]}
( 10... Nc4?!
{11.Bxc4 dxc4 hands White the d5 square and leaves Black with a weak pawn on c4.} )
11. Bd3
{White develops and steps the queen out from behind the rook's line of fire. [%weight 6]}
11... Be7
{Modest development. With a bishop on f4 already, ...Bd6 only invites a trade that helps White. [%tags development king-safety]}
12. O-O
{White castles, a couple of moves behind where the opening should have left the queen. [%weight 7]}
12... O-O
{Castle. Black has the file, the better minor piece plan and no weaknesses. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Bring the knight from a5 to c4, put the second rook on c8, and hit b2 with the queen once the dark-squared bishops come off.] [%line White's 7.Qb3 hit on b7]}
( 12... Nc4!?
{Tempting, and 13.Bxc4 dxc4 gives White the d5 square. Castle first and take on c4 with a piece later.}
) ) ( 7. Nf3 {Straightforward development, leaving the queen where she is. [%weight 5]}
7... e6
{Solid, and now the position is the same one the 7.Bd3 line reaches a move later. [%tags development] [%plan Play ...Bd6 to offer the bishop trade, castle, and put a rook on c8. This is the main line of the chapter by another move order.] [%line The quiet 7.Nf3]}
( 7... Nb4?!
{8.Rc1 and the knight has nowhere useful to go. Black has no reason to hurry.} ) )
7... Bxd3
{Take. Avoiding the trade would mean retreating, and the trade costs Black nothing here. [%tags bishop-placement]}
( 7... Bg6!?
{Keeping the bishop is fine, and 8.Bxg6 hxg6 makes the trade happen anyway on White's terms.}
) 8. Qxd3 {White recaptures and the queen sits on a reasonable square. [%weight 9]}
8... e6
{Now the pawn goes to e6. With the light-squared bishops off there is nothing left to shut in. [%tags development]}
9. Nf3 {White finishes developing the minor pieces. [%weight 7]} 9... Bd6
{Offer the trade of dark-squared bishops. Once they are off, b2 is a real target for the queen. [%tags bishop-placement]}
( 9... Be7!?
{Solid, and it leaves White's bishop the better of the two. On d6 it asks a question.} ) (
9... Bb4?!
{There is nothing to pin: the queen has left d1 and the knight on c3 is defended twice.} )
10. Bxd6
{White accepts the trade rather than retreat the bishop to a worse square. [%weight 6]}
10... Qxd6
{Recapture with the queen, which now eyes b4 and the whole dark-squared diagonal. [%tags dark-squares]}
11. O-O {White castles into a level position with nothing much to do. [%weight 7]}
11... O-O
{Castle and get the rooks ready. Black's plan is the c-file and it starts now. [%tags king-safety]}
12. Rfc1
{White claims the file first, which is the right idea and one move too slow to matter. [%weight 6]}
12... Rfc8
{Contest the file at once. Whoever gets a rook to c8 or c1 without being traded off has the better game. [%tags c-file] [%plan Trade one pair of rooks on the c-file and keep the other. Then bring the knight to a5 and on to c4, and hit b2 with the queen once White's pieces are tied down.] [%line The full main line, with 7.Bd3]}
( 12... Rac8!?
{Also fine. Using the rook that has no other job keeps the a-file rook for the queenside pawns.}
) ( 12... e5?!
{11.dxe5 Nxe5 12.Nxe5 Qxe5 leaves Black with an isolated pawn on d5 and no compensation for it.}
) *


[CourseId "slav-black"]
[CourseTitle "The Slav Defence"]
[StudentColour "black"]
[Tagline "One structure against 1.d4, with the bishop outside, as Black."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "vs-quiet vs-exchange-slav vs-catalan vs-london light-squares c5-break e5-break c-file"]
[ChapterId "ch4-sidelines"]
[ChapterTitle "Sidelines and move-order tricks"]
[DefaultTags "development"]

{Everything else White can do with the c4 pawn on the board: a different knight order, an early queen move, or a fianchetto. Most of it transposes back into the first three chapters, and the value of this chapter is knowing which one you have landed in.

## The rule that decides everything here

There is only one order question in the whole opening: is ...Bf5 safe yet. It is safe when White cannot answer cxd5 and Qb3 with real effect, and that means either the c-file is already resolved or Black has a piece covering b7. Get that right and White's move orders are just different roads to the same four positions.

## Which chapter you are in

3.Nc3 followed by Nf3 is chapter 1: take on c4. 3.Nc3 or 3.e3 followed by e3 is chapter 2: play ...Bf5 and develop. Any cxd5 is chapter 3: recapture with the c-pawn and take the file. Only the fianchetto lines are genuinely their own thing, and they are the reason this chapter exists.

## Against g3 and the Catalan look

When White fianchettoes, the bishop on g2 is aimed straight at b7 and d5. That makes ...Bf5 more valuable, not less, because Black wants the light squares defended by a piece rather than by pawns. Develop with ...Bf5, ...e6, ...Nbd7 and ...Be7, and answer Nh4 by letting the bishop be traded and recapturing on g6 with the h-pawn.

## Against an early queen move

4.Qc2 is met by 4...dxc4. The queen has to spend a move recapturing, and while she does it Black gets the bishop out to f5 with gain of time later. This is the same bargain as the main line, offered on better terms.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Playing ...Bf5 before the knight is out, and running into cxd5 followed by Qb3. Grabbing on c4 against 3.Nc3 before a knight is developed, when 4.e4 gives White the whole centre. Treating a transposition as a new opening and inventing a plan for it. And answering the fianchetto with ...e6 first, which leaves Black passive against exactly the bishop that wants Black passive.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3
{The other knight first. It keeps e4 in reserve and can catch Black out if the pawn is taken too early. [%weight 6]}
( 3. e3
{The quietest move order of all. White has not committed a knight and has already shut the bishop in. [%weight 4]}
3... Nf6 {Develop the knight first. There is no hurry and no threat to answer.} (
3... Bf5!?
{Also good here, because White has no quick Qb3. Bringing the knight out first never costs anything.}
) 4. Nf3
{White reaches the Slow Slav, which is the first half of chapter 2. [%weight 7] [%plan Play ...Bf5 at once, then ...e6, ...Nbd7 and a bishop to d6.] [%line 3.e3 into the main line]}
( 4. Nc3
{The same position as 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.e3 above, reached the other way round. [%weight 5] [%plan Play ...Bf5, then ...e6 and ordinary development. Nothing in the position has changed with the move order.] [%line 3.e3 and then 4.Nc3]}
) ) ( 3. g3
{A Catalan look. The bishop on g2 will aim at b7 and d5, which are the squares this whole opening is about. [%weight 3]}
3... Nf6 {Develop and keep everything defended before deciding where the bishop goes.} (
3... dxc4!?
{Grabbing the pawn is playable and it hands the bishop on g2 the long diagonal it wants. Developing first is simpler.}
) 4. Bg2
{White finishes the fianchetto and points the bishop at Black's queenside. [%weight 8]}
4... Bf5
{The light squares need a piece, not a pawn. From f5 the bishop answers the one on g2. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares vs-catalan]}
( 4... e6?!
{It leaves the light squares to the bishop on g2 and buries Black's own bishop. This is the position White fianchettoed for.}
) ( 4... dxc4!?
{Also possible, and the diagonal opens for the bishop on g2 straight away. Holding the centre is the calmer choice.}
) 5. Nf3 {White develops and prepares to castle. [%weight 6]} ( 5. cxd5
{White opens the diagonal for the bishop on g2 and prepares Qb3. [%weight 4]} 5... cxd5
{Take with the c-pawn. The file is worth more than the shape of the centre. [%tags c-file]}
6. Qb3
{The familiar double attack on b7 and d5, this time with a bishop behind it. [%weight 7]}
6... Qb6
{The familiar answer. Offering the trade solves b7 and d5 in one move, bishop on g2 or not. [%tags tactics] [%plan Trade queens if White allows it, then play ...e6, ...Nbd7 and ...Be7. With the queens off, the bishop on g2 has nothing left to aim at.] [%line 3.g3 and the 5.cxd5 trade]}
( 6... Qc7?!
{7.Bf4 comes with a hit on the queen and White keeps the pull. It does not lose a pawn, because 7.Qxd5 would run into 7...Qxc1+, but the queen is misplaced on c7 here.}
) ( 6... b6?!
{It defends b7 with a pawn and leaves c6 and a6 full of holes for a bishop on g2 to use. Offering the queen trade solves the whole problem.}
) ) 5... e6 {Now the pawn is safe to play, with the bishop already outside it.} 6. O-O
{White castles into a comfortable, slow position. [%weight 7]} 6... Nbd7
{Develop and defend c5 and e5. The knight also clears the way for the queen to defend d5 again.}
7. Nc3 {The last minor piece, adding a third attacker to d5. [%weight 5]} 7... Be7
{Quiet development. Black has no weaknesses and White has no way in. [%tags development king-safety]}
( 7... dxc4!?
{Also good, and the engine prefers it: taking makes White spend a move on the pawn. Finishing the set-up first is the simpler way to play the position.}
) 8. Nh4
{The standard plan against ...Bf5: two knight moves to trade the bishop off. [%weight 6]}
8... Bg6
{Let it happen. The bishop has already earned its keep by making White spend the time. [%tags bishop-placement]}
9. Nxg6 {White carries out the trade. [%weight 8]} 9... hxg6
{Take with the h-pawn: the rook gets a file and the structure stays sound. [%tags h-file]}
( 9... fxg6?
{It leaves a hole on e6 with a bishop on g2 already looking at it, and gives up the h-file.}
) 10. e3 {White shores up d4 before doing anything else. [%weight 6]} 10... O-O
{Castle. Black is solid, fully developed and has a half-open file to work with. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Play ...Rc8 and think about ...dxc4 followed by ...c5, which frees the position while the bishop on g2 still has nothing to bite on.] [%line The 3.g3 Catalan setup]}
( 10... dxc4!?
{Also reasonable, and it opens the long diagonal for the bishop on g2 before Black is ready for it. Castling first costs nothing.}
) ) ( 3. Nf3
{The main developing move. What follows here is everything except 4.Nc3, 4.e3 and 4.Bf4. [%weight 8]}
3... Nf6 {Develop, cover e4 and keep the tension on d5 for one more move.} ( 3... Bf5?!
{One move too early. 4.cxd5 cxd5 5.Qb3 hits b7 and d5 at once, and the bishop has just stopped guarding b7.}
) 4. Qc2
{An early queen move, keeping the pawn on c4 defended without blocking anything. [%weight 4]}
( 4. g3 {The fianchetto with both knights still flexible. [%weight 4]} 4... Bf5
{Get the bishop out before the one on g2 arrives to argue about the light squares. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares vs-catalan]}
( 4... e6?!
{It hands the light squares to White's bishop and shuts Black's in. That is the whole point of the fianchetto.}
) 5. Bg2 {White completes the set-up. [%weight 8]} 5... e6
{Now it is safe, and it gives the dark-squared bishop a path.} 6. O-O
{White castles and waits for Black to commit. [%weight 7]} 6... Nbd7
{Develop and cover the central squares before the bishops get busy.} 7. Nbd2
{A quiet set-up, keeping the pawn on c4 defended by the queen later rather than by a knight now. [%weight 5]}
7... Be7 {Finish the development and get ready to castle. [%tags development king-safety]}
( 7... dxc4!?
{Also good, and the engine slightly prefers it here as well: taking makes White spend a move on the pawn. Finishing the set-up is the simpler way to play it.}
) 8. b3 {White props up c4 and prepares to develop the last bishop. [%weight 5]} 8... O-O
{Castle. Black has a solid triangle of pawns and every piece on a sensible square. [%tags king-safety]}
9. Bb2 {The last piece comes out and White is fully developed. [%weight 7]} 9... Ne4
{The knight takes the outpost and stops White from ever getting Ne5 in comfortably. [%plan Follow with ...f5 to hold the knight on e4, or trade on d2 and play ...c5. The one thing to avoid is drifting while White prepares e4.] [%line 3.Nf3 into a Catalan]}
( 9... c5!?
{Also right in spirit, and 10.cxd5 exd5 11.dxc5 leaves Black with an isolated pawn. Improving the knight first is safer.}
) ) ( 4. cxd5 {The delayed Exchange, with both knights already out. [%weight 4]} 4... cxd5
{Take with the c-pawn. This is chapter 3 with a pair of knights already developed. [%tags c-file]}
( 4... Nxd5?!
{5.e4 hits the knight and takes the centre. The pawn is the right recapture every time here.}
) 5. Nc3 {White develops and adds an attacker to d5. [%weight 7]} 5... Nc6
{Develop and press d4, exactly as in chapter 3.} 6. Bf4
{The bishop takes the good diagonal before e3 shuts it in. [%weight 6]} 6... Bf5
{Answer in kind. The bishops mirror each other and the position is dead level. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares]}
7. e3 {White finishes the small centre. [%weight 7]} 7... e6
{Solid and symmetrical. Black's plan from here is the c-file and nothing else.} 8. Bd3
{White offers the trade of light-squared bishops. [%weight 6]} 8... Bxd3
{Take. The bishop has done its work and there is nothing to gain by dodging. [%tags bishop-placement]}
9. Qxd3 {White recaptures with the queen, which is the only piece that can. [%weight 9]}
9... Bd6
{Offer the second trade. With both pairs of bishops gone, b2 becomes something to aim at. [%tags bishop-placement]}
10. Bxd6 {White takes rather than retreat to a worse square. [%weight 6]} 10... Qxd6
{Recapture with the queen, who now watches the dark squares White has just given up. [%tags dark-squares] [%plan Castle, put a rook on c8 and play ...Na5 followed by ...Nc4. The symmetry is only a draw if Black lets it be one.] [%line 3.Nf3 into the Exchange]}
) 4... dxc4
{Take. The queen has to spend a move recapturing, which is the same bargain as the main line on better terms. [%tags tactics]}
( 4... e6!?
{Solid, and it lets White keep everything. Taking makes the queen work for the pawn.} ) (
4... Bf5?
{5.Qxf5 wins a bishop. The queen went to c2 for exactly this diagonal, which is the point of putting her there.}
) 5. Qxc4
{White recovers the pawn, at the cost of a queen move that Black is about to gain time on. [%weight 9]}
5... Bf5
{Out, with the queen already committed. The bishop has a clean diagonal and nothing to fear. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares]}
6. g3
{White fianchettoes, which is the usual set-up when the queen has taken on c4. [%weight 5]}
6... e6 {Solid and safe, with the bishop already outside.} 7. Bg2
{The bishop takes the long diagonal and eyes b7 and d5. [%weight 8]} 7... Nbd7
{Develop and cover c5 and e5, the squares White's pieces would like.} 8. O-O
{White castles and the position is quiet and balanced. [%weight 7]} 8... Be7
{Modest development, keeping the dark squares around the king covered. [%tags development king-safety]}
9. Nc3 {White finishes developing. [%weight 6]} 9... O-O
{Castle. Every piece is out and Black has nothing to defend. [%tags king-safety]} 10. e3
{White shores up d4 and waits. [%weight 5]} 10... Rc8
{Take the file the pawn on c6 left behind. Black's whole plan in this line is the c-file and the ...c5 break. [%tags c-file] [%plan Play ...Qb6 or ...Qa5 to add pressure, then ...c5. Once the c-file is open and Black's rook is on it first, the bishop on g2 is biting on granite.] [%line 3.Nf3 and the 4.Qc2 setup]}
( 10... c5?!
{Too early. 11.dxc5 Bxc5 12.Qe2 leaves White with the freer game and Black with a pawn on d5 to look after. Put the rook on the file first.}
) ) 3... Nf6
{Develop first. Taking on c4 now would let White build the centre with tempo.} (
3... dxc4?!
{4.e4 gives White the whole centre and a big lead in development, and Black has to hold the pawn with ...b5 to show for it.}
) ( 3... e6!?
{The Semi-Slav again, and it shuts in the bishop on c8. This repertoire keeps the diagonal open.}
) 4. Nf3
{White transposes into the main line of the whole opening. [%weight 7] [%plan This is exactly chapter 1: take on c4 and meet a4 with ...Bf5.] [%line 3.Nc3 into the main line]}
( 4. e3
{The quiet build-up with the knights placed differently, and it comes to the same thing. [%weight 5]}
4... Bf5
{Out it goes. With the pawn on e3 White has no way to punish it, exactly as in chapter 2. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares]}
( 4... e6!? {Solid, and it gives up the free move that 4.e3 handed Black.} ) 5. Nf3
{White develops and the position is now word for word the one chapter 2 reaches. [%weight 7] [%plan Play ...e6, then ...Nbd7 and a bishop to d6, and meet Nh4 by recapturing on g6 with the h-pawn.] [%line 3.Nc3 and the quiet 4.e3]}
) ( 4. cxd5
{White exchanges with the knights already out, which is the Exchange Slav a move later. [%weight 5]}
4... cxd5
{Take with the c-pawn, as always. The file matters more than the extra centre pawn would. [%tags c-file]}
( 4... Nxd5?!
{5.e4 hits the knight and takes the centre, and Black's pawn on c6 is left doing nothing.}
) 5. Bf4 {The usual square for the bishop in this structure. [%weight 6]} 5... Nc6
{Develop and press d4. Black is one move from the main position of chapter 3.} 6. e3
{White finishes the small centre, and this is the Exchange Slav from chapter 3 exactly. [%weight 7] [%plan Play ...Bf5, then ...e6 and ...Bd6, castle, and take the c-file. If Qb3 comes, the answer is still ...Na5.] [%line 3.Nc3 into the Exchange]}
) *


[CourseId "slav-black"]
[CourseTitle "The Slav Defence"]
[StudentColour "black"]
[Tagline "One structure against 1.d4, with the bishop outside, as Black."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "vs-quiet vs-exchange-slav vs-catalan vs-london light-squares c5-break e5-break c-file"]
[ChapterId "ch5-no-c4"]
[ChapterTitle "White without c4: the London, Colle and Torre"]
[DefaultTags "development"]

{White plays a system: the same four or five moves whatever Black does. These are the most common openings you will meet at club level and none of them fights for d5, which changes what Black should do.

## The one place this course changes its mind

Everywhere else Black plays ...c6 and holds d5. Here, do not. The pawn on c6 exists to answer c4, and if White never plays c4 then ...c6 is a wasted move that leaves Black passive with nothing to do. Play ...c5 instead and hit the centre while White is still building.

The bishop rule survives intact, and it matters more here than anywhere. Against the Colle in particular, ...e6 before the bishop is out is the exact position White is playing for.

## The London

After 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 c5 4.Nf3 Nc6 5.c3 Qb6 the queen hits b2, which the bishop left when it went to f4. White's usual answer is 6.Qb3, offering the trade. Do not take. Play 6...c4! and the queen is trapped into taking on b6 instead, after which Black has the a-file, a bind on the queenside and doubled b-pawns that nothing can reach.

One warning, and it is the reason the move order above is what it is. Playing ...c5 and ...Qb6 before ...Nf6 invites Nc3, when Qxb2 runs into Nb5 and White is doing very well. Develop the knight first and there is no gambit to walk into.

## The Colle

2.Nf3 Nf6 3.e3 c5 4.c3 Nc6 5.Bd3 and White wants to play Nbd2, O-O and then e4, with the bishop on d3 aimed at h7. Answer 5...Bg4. Once the bishop is outside, the e4 break loses its point: when it comes, Black trades on e4 and then removes the knight on f3 with ...Bxf3 before hitting d4.

## The Torre and the Jobava

3.Bg5 is met by 3...Ne4, gaining time on the bishop. 2.Nc3 followed by Bf4 needs 3...a6 first, because Nb5 hits c7, and only then ...Bf5.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Playing the Slav structure with ...c6 anyway, and drifting into a passive position with no break. Grabbing on b2 with the queen when Nb5 is available. Playing ...e6 before the bishop against the Colle, which is the whole point of White's system. And taking the queen trade on b3 when ...c4 keeps far more life in the position.}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4
{The London. White will play e3, c3, Nf3, Bd3 and Nbd2 almost regardless of what Black does. [%weight 7]}
( 2. Nf3
{The flexible move. It can become a Colle, a Torre, a London or a real Queen's Gambit next move. [%weight 6]}
2... Nf6
{Develop and wait. Black commits nothing until White says what the game is going to be.} (
2... c5!?
{Also good and it invites 3.c4, which is a different opening altogether. Developing first keeps the Slav available.}
) 3. e3
{The Colle. White plans Bd3, Nbd2, O-O and then e4, with the bishop aimed at h7. [%weight 6]}
( 3. Bg5
{The Torre. White pins nothing yet and hopes to trade the bishop for the knight on f6. [%weight 4]}
3... Ne4
{Hit the bishop at once. It has to decide, and every square it picks costs White a move. [%tags tactics]}
( 3... e6!?
{Solid, and it walks into the Torre's main line where White is comfortable and Black's bishop is not.}
) ( 3... c5!?
{Also principled, and 4.Bxf6 gxf6 or 4.e3 both give White a normal game. Gaining time on the bishop first is better.}
) 4. Bf4 {The bishop keeps the good diagonal and stays out of the pawn chain. [%weight 6]}
( 4. Bh4
{The bishop stays on the diagonal and keeps an eye on the knight's retreat squares. [%weight 4]}
4... c5 {Hit the centre while the bishop is off doing something else. [%tags c5-break]}
5. e3
{White props up d4 and the game settles into the same shape as the 4.Bf4 line. [%weight 7] [%plan Play ...Nc6 and ...Qb6 as usual, and remember the bishop on h4 is not defending b2 either.] [%line The Torre, with 4.Bh4]}
) 4... c5
{Now hit the centre, with a knight already sitting on e4 and White a move behind. [%tags c5-break]}
5. e3 {White shores up d4 and gets ready to develop. [%weight 7]} 5... Nc6
{Develop and add another attacker to d4.} 6. c3
{The usual prop, and it leaves b2 unguarded as usual too. [%weight 5]} 6... Qb6
{The same hit on b2, and with the bishop on f4 rather than c1 it is a real one. The threat is worth more than the pawn, so do not rush to take it. [%tags dark-squares]}
( 6... cxd4?!
{7.exd4 gives White a healthy centre and releases all the pressure before the queen has asked her question.}
) 7. Qc1
{An awkward square, and it is the move that holds everything together. [%weight 5]}
7... Bf5
{The last minor piece comes out, and it holds the knight on e4 in place. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares]}
8. Nbd2 {White finally develops and challenges the knight on e4. [%weight 6]} 8... e6
{Solid, with both bishops already outside the chain.} 9. Nxe4
{White trades before the knight becomes permanent. [%weight 5]} 9... Bxe4
{Recapture with the bishop, which lands on a square nothing can challenge. [%tags light-squares]}
10. Be2 {White develops and prepares to castle out of the middle. [%weight 6]} 10... Be7
{Finish the development. Black has the two bishops pointing at a queenside White has not organised. [%tags development king-safety] [%plan Castle, then play ...Rfd8 and ...cxd4 at a moment when the recapture leaves White with a weak pawn. The bishop on e4 is the best piece on the board.] [%line The Torre, with 4.Bf4]}
( 10... cxd4?!
{Too early. 11.exd4 or 11.cxd4 both give White a healthy centre while Black's king is still in the middle.}
) ) ( 3. Bf4
{The London by another route, with the knight already on f3. [%weight 5] [%plan Play ...c5 and follow the London lines above: ...Nc6, ...Qb6, and ...c4 against Qb3.] [%line 2.Nf3 into the London]}
) 3... c5
{Hit d4 before White is set up. The Colle needs a quiet centre and this refuses to give one. [%tags c5-break]}
( 3... e6?!
{This is the position White is playing for. The bishop on c8 watches the whole game from behind its own pawn.}
) ( 3... Bf5!?
{Also sensible, and 4.c4 turns the game back into a Slav where Black has committed the bishop early.}
) 4. c3
{The Colle proper. White builds the little pyramid and prepares Bd3 and e4. [%weight 7]}
4... Nc6
{Develop and press d4. The pressure is what stops White from getting e4 in for free.} (
4... Qb6!?
{Less effective here than against the London, because the bishop on c1 has not moved and still guards b2.}
) 5. dxc5
{The critical try, and the one a Colle player rarely finds. White grabs the pawn and hopes to hold it with b4. [%weight 3]}
( 5. Bd3 {The Colle bishop. It points at h7 and waits for the centre to open. [%weight 8]}
5... Bg4
{Out before ...e6, and this square rather than f5 because the knight on f3 is the piece holding d4 together. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares]}
( 5... e6?!
{The Colle exists for this position. White plays Nbd2, O-O and e4 with an attack, and Black's bishop never gets out.}
) ( 5... Bf5?
{6.Bxf5 simply wins a bishop: with no pawn on e6 and none on g6, nothing takes back. On g4 the bishop is safe and it hits the defender of d4.}
) 6. Nbd2
{White develops the last knight towards e4, which is the whole plan. [%weight 7]} 6... e6
{Now the pawn is safe to play, and it opens the way for the bishop on f8.} 7. O-O
{White castles and gets ready for the break. [%weight 7]} 7... Bd6
{The bishop takes the diagonal pointing at White's king, opposite the one on d3. [%tags bishop-placement]}
( 7... Be7!?
{Solid and passive. On d6 the bishop is aimed at h2, which matters the moment White's knight leaves f3.}
) 8. Re1 {White prepares e4 by putting a rook behind the pawn. [%weight 6]} 8... O-O
{Castle and let the break come. Black is ready for it and White is not. [%tags king-safety] [%plan When e4 comes, take on e4 and trade the knights. Then remove the knight on f3 with ...Bxf3 before hitting d4 with ...cxd4, and the pawn on d4 falls.] [%line The Colle, with 5.Bd3]}
( 8... cxd4?!
{9.cxd4 hands White the centre and gives up the pressure that makes this position comfortable.}
) ) 5... e5
{Take the centre instead of the pawn. With pawns on d5 and e5 Black has more space than White has extra material. [%tags e5-break] [%plan Play ...Be7 or ...Bxc5, castle, and only then decide about the pawn. If White holds it with b4, the queenside squares White gave up are worth more than the pawn.] [%line The Colle, with 5.dxc5]}
( 5... Qa5+?!
{6.Nc3 or 6.Bd2 blocks and the queen has to move again. The pawn is not running anywhere.}
) ) ( 2. e3
{A quiet start that usually becomes a Colle. White has committed nothing except the bishop on c1. [%weight 3]}
2... Nf6 {Develop and see what White is building.} 3. Bd3
{The Colle bishop comes out before the knight, which changes nothing important. [%weight 6]}
3... c5 {Hit the centre as always against a system opening. [%tags c5-break]} 4. c3
{White builds the pyramid. [%weight 6]} 4... Nc6 {Develop and press d4.} 5. Nf3
{White is now in the Colle from the section above, by a different order. [%weight 7] [%plan Play ...Bg4 at once, before ...e6. That is the move the whole Colle set-up is designed to prevent.] [%line The 2.e3 move order]}
) ( 2. Nc3
{The Jobava set-up when it is followed by Bf4. It is sharper than the ordinary London and more common every year. [%weight 3]}
2... Nf6 {Develop and stop e4 in its tracks.} 3. Bf4
{The Jobava London. The knight on c3 supports a quick Nb5, which is what makes it different. [%weight 7]}
3... a6
{Take b5 away first. It looks slow and it is the move that makes the rest of Black's set-up possible. [%tags vs-london]}
( 3... Bf5?!
{4.Nb5 hits c7 straight away and Black spends the next few moves untangling. Spend one move on ...a6 and the problem never arises.}
) ( 3... e6!?
{Solid, and it shuts in the bishop before it has had a chance. With ...a6 first, the bishop still gets to f5.}
) 4. e3 {White settles into the usual structure. [%weight 6]} 4... Bf5
{Now the bishop can come out, because Nb5 is no longer available to hit c7. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares]}
5. Nf3 {White develops and prepares to castle. [%weight 6]} 5... e6
{Solid, with the bishop already outside the chain.} 6. Bd3
{White offers the trade of light-squared bishops. [%weight 6]} 6... Bxd3
{Take. Black's bishop has done its job and White's had a future. [%tags bishop-placement]}
7. Qxd3 {The queen recaptures, since nothing else can. [%weight 9]} 7... Bd6
{Offer the second trade. The bishop on f4 is White's best piece and it is worth swapping. [%tags bishop-placement]}
8. Bxd6 {White takes rather than retreat to a worse square. [%weight 6]} 8... Qxd6
{Recapture with the queen, who takes over the dark squares White has given up. [%tags dark-squares]}
9. O-O {White castles into a level, simplified position. [%weight 7]} 9... Nbd7
{Develop the last piece towards the centre, covering c5 and e5.} 10. Rac1
{White takes the half-open file, which is the one thing to do here. [%weight 5]} 10... O-O
{Castle. Black has no weaknesses and a clear plan, which is more than White has. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Play ...c5 to open the centre while White's rook sits on a file that is about to be blocked, then use the d5 pawn's extra space.] [%line The Jobava London]}
( 10... b5?!
{It weakens c5 and c6 with a rook already on the c-file. Castle and break in the centre instead.}
) ) 2... Nf6
{Develop first. The knight has to come out anyway and it takes the sting out of an early Nc3. [%tags development vs-london]}
( 2... c5!?
{Also good, and 3.e3 Qb6 4.Nc3! is the gambit a prepared London player wants: 4...Qxb2 5.Nb5 and White is doing very well.}
) ( 2... e6?!
{The passive set-up the London is built to beat. It shuts in the bishop and gives White the whole plan for free.}
) ( 2... Bf5!?
{Sensible in a Slav sort of way, and it does nothing about White's centre. Hitting d4 with ...c5 is the sharper answer.}
) 3. e3
{The standard follow-up. White props up d4, and in doing so shuts in the bishop that is still on c1 in most openings. [%weight 8]}
( 3. Nf3
{White develops before e3, which is a slightly different order to the same set-up. [%weight 4]}
3... c5
{Hit the centre as always. The order White chooses does not change what Black is doing. [%tags c5-break vs-london]}
4. e3
{White is back in the main line of this chapter, one move order later. [%weight 8] [%plan Play ...Nc6, then ...Qb6 hitting b2, and answer Qb3 with ...c4 rather than the trade.] [%line The London, 3.Nf3 first]}
) 3... c5
{Hit the centre. White has not fought for d5, so Black takes a free hand and attacks d4 instead. [%tags c5-break vs-london]}
( 3... c6?!
{The Slav move without the Slav. With no pawn on c4 to answer, ...c6 leaves Black solid, passive and short of a plan.}
) ( 3... e6?!
{It shuts the bishop in and lets White carry out the whole system unopposed.} ) 4. Nf3
{White develops before committing the c-pawn, which is the careful order. [%weight 7]} (
4. c3
{White commits the c-pawn first, which lets Black hit b2 a move earlier. [%weight 5]}
4... Qb6
{The same idea, and here it comes with the knight on b1 still at home so there is no Nc3 gambit to fear. [%tags dark-squares vs-london]}
( 4... Nc6!?
{Also good and it usually transposes. The queen move asks its question straight away.} )
5. Qb3 {White defends by offering the trade, as usual. [%weight 6]} 5... c4
{Push past instead of trading. White's queen has to take on b6 and Black gets the file and the bind. [%tags c5-break vs-london]}
6. Qxb6 {Forced in practice, and it is exactly what Black was hoping for. [%weight 7]}
6... axb6 {Recapture towards the centre and open the a-file. [%tags c-file]} 7. Nd2
{White develops and eyes b3, the one way to challenge the pawn on c4. [%weight 6]}
7... Nc6
{Develop with the pawn chain already set. The knight heads for a5 to hold c4 in place. [%plan Play ...Bf5, ...e6 and ...Be7, castle, and answer b3 with ...Na5 so the pawn on c4 stays where it is.] [%line The London, with 4.c3]}
( 7... b5?!
{It leaves the pawn on c4 with one defender fewer and hands White the a5 square. Bring the knight out first.}
) ) ( 4. dxc5
{White grabs the pawn, which gives up the centre and usually gives the pawn back a move later. [%weight 3]}
4... e6
{Open the path to c5 and take the pawn back next move with the bishop, fully developed. [%tags c5-break] [%plan Recapture with ...Bxc5 next move. If White tries to hold the pawn with b4, hit the chain with ...a5 before anything else.] [%line The London, with 4.dxc5]}
( 4... Qa5+?!
{5.Nc3 or 5.c3 blocks the check and the queen has to move again. The pawn is not running away.}
) ) 4... Nc6
{Develop and add a second attacker to d4. Every Black piece in this line points at the same pawn. [%tags development vs-london]}
( 4... Qb6!?
{The same idea a move early, and 5.Nc3 is then a real option for White. Bringing the knight out first takes that away.}
) ( 4... cxd4?!
{5.exd4 hands White a healthy centre and the open e-file, and releases all the pressure for nothing.}
) 5. c3
{White props up d4 a third time. The cost is that b2 now has nothing defending it. [%weight 7]}
5... Qb6
{The critical move. The queen hits b2, which the bishop stopped guarding when it went to f4. [%tags dark-squares vs-london]}
( 5... Bf5!?
{Sound development, and it lets White finish the system in peace. ...Qb6 is the move that asks a question.}
) ( 5... e6!?
{Solid, and it gives up the one moment when b2 is undefended and Black is fully developed on the queenside.}
) 6. Qb3
{The standard answer. White defends b2 by offering the trade, expecting Black to take. [%weight 7]}
6... c4
{Do not take. The pawn push gains space, shuts the bishop on f1 out of the game and forces White to trade queens on Black's terms. [%tags c5-break vs-london]}
( 6... Qxb3?!
{The trade White wants and is prepared for: 7.axb3 gives White the open a-file and a comfortable, slightly better game.}
) 7. Qxb6
{White has little choice. Retreating the queen would leave her worse than she was two moves ago. [%weight 7]}
7... axb6
{Take towards the centre and open the a-file. The doubled pawn on b6 is safe, and the rook on a8 is already working. [%tags c-file]}
8. Nbd2
{White develops and prepares to challenge the pawn on c4 with b3 later. [%weight 6]}
8... Bf5
{Out it comes, on the diagonal White's own bishop left when it went to f4. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares]}
( 8... b5?!
{It fixes the queenside too early and gives White the a5 square. Develop first and push later.}
) 9. Be2
{The bishop has nowhere better. On d3 it would be traded and on f1 it does nothing. [%weight 5]}
9... e6 {Solid, and safe now that the bishop is already outside the chain.} 10. O-O
{White castles into a position with no obvious plan in it. [%weight 7]} 10... Be7
{Finish developing. Black has the space, the file and the better long-term chances. [%tags development king-safety] [%plan Castle, double on the a-file and play ...b5 and ...b4 when the rooks are ready. White's counter is b3; meet it by keeping the pawn on c4 defended.] [%line The London, with 6.Qb3]}
( 10... b5!?
{The right idea too early. Get the king out of the middle before starting a pawn advance.}
) *


[CourseId "slav-black"]
[CourseTitle "The Slav Defence"]
[StudentColour "black"]
[Tagline "One structure against 1.d4, with the bishop outside, as Black."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "vs-quiet vs-exchange-slav vs-catalan vs-london light-squares c5-break e5-break c-file"]
[ChapterId "ch6-tactics-and-plans"]
[ChapterTitle "Typical tactics and plans"]
[DefaultTags "tactics"]

{Five positions from the lines you have just learnt, each stopping on the move that matters. Play through the moves and find the last one.

## What these are, and what they are not

Three of the five end on a genuine tactic: a bishop that cannot be captured, a knight that defends a pawn by attacking it, and a pin that stays invisible until you move the piece holding it shut. The other two end on a decision, which is the more common kind of moment in a real game. Knowing that ...c4 is better than the queen trade will win you more games than any combination in this opening, and it is not a tactic.

## The themes worth carrying away

An open file is a tactic waiting to happen. In the quiet lines Black recaptures on g6 with the h-pawn for ordinary structural reasons, and eight moves later that rook makes ...Bxh2+ unanswerable, because a king cannot capture a defended piece.

Defend by attacking. In the Exchange Slav, ...Na5 guards b7 by attacking b7, so the queen that was going to take there would lose herself. The follow-up matters just as much: the check on a4 has to be blocked by the bishop, because only the bishop attacks the queen back.

Watch the diagonal behind your own piece. In the 6.Ne5 line Black's knight on e5 is pinned against the queen on c7, and the pin is invisible until you move the knight and lose the queen.

Space before trades. Against the London, pushing the pawn to c4 keeps a queenside bind that a queen trade on b3 hands straight back.

Strike the wedge. When White's pawn reaches e5 in the main line, ...f6 is the move that stops the position going quietly downhill.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Playing a sensible developing move in a position that has a forcing one in it. Trading queens because the trade was offered. Moving a piece that is holding a diagonal shut. And reaching a comfortable position and then having no plan, which is the most expensive mistake on this list.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4
{The Queen's Gambit, which is where four of the five exercises start. [%weight 9]} (
2. Bf4
{Exercise five, the London. Play to move six and choose between the queen trade and something better. [%weight 7]}
2... Nf6
{Develop first, so that Nc3 has no gambit behind it. [%tags development vs-london]} 3. e3
{White props up d4 and shuts in the bishop that has already left. [%weight 8]} 3... c5
{Hit the centre, because White has not fought for d5. [%tags c5-break vs-london]} 4. Nf3
{White develops. [%weight 7]} 4... Nc6
{A second attacker on d4. [%tags development vs-london]} 5. c3
{White props d4 up a third time and leaves b2 with nothing on it. [%weight 7]} 5... Qb6
{Hit b2. [%tags dark-squares vs-london]} 6. Qb3
{White offers the trade. Black to play: taking is safe, comfortable and exactly what White prepared for. What is better? [%weight 7]}
6... c4
{Push past. The queen has to take on b6 on Black's terms, and the pawn on c4 shuts the bishop on f1 out of the game for the rest of it. [%tags c5-break vs-london] [%plan Recapture on b6 with the a-pawn, then ...Bf5, ...e6 and ...Be7. The plan is the a-file and a later ...b5 and ...b4.] [%line The London: the ...c4 clamp]}
( 6... Qxb3?!
{Safe and level, and 7.axb3 gives White the open a-file and the more comfortable game. This is the trade a London player has seen a hundred times.}
) ( 6... cxd4?!
{7.exd4 or 7.Qxb6 both leave White comfortable, and the queenside bind that ...c4 creates is gone for good.}
) ) 2... c6 {The Slav. [%tags development]} 3. Nf3 {The main move. [%weight 8]} ( 3. cxd5
{Exercise four, the Exchange Slav. Play to move seven and find the answer to the queen on b3. [%weight 6]}
3... cxd5 {Recapture with the c-pawn. [%tags c-file]} 4. Nc3 {White develops. [%weight 6]}
4... Nf6 {Develop and hold d5. [%tags development]} 5. Bf4
{The bishop goes outside the chain. [%weight 6]} 5... Nc6
{Develop and press d4. [%tags development]} 6. e3 {White props up d4. [%weight 6]}
6... Bf5 {Out before ...e6, as always. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares]} 7. Qb3
{The queen hits b7 and d5 together. Black to play: b7 has no defender and d5 has exactly one. What holds both? [%weight 5]}
7... Na5
{Defend by attacking. The knight covers b7 by attacking it, so 8.Qxb7 loses the queen to 8...Nxb7, and White has to check on a4 instead. [%plan Meet 8.Qa4+ with 8...Bd7, gaining another move, and bring the knight back into the game through c4 or c6 once the queen has gone home.] [%line The Exchange: the ...Na5 hit]}
( 7... Qd7?!
{It defends b7 and ties the queen to a pawn. The knight does the same job and gains a move as well.}
) ( 7... b6?
{8.Ba6 takes the light squares ...b6 has just given up, and Black's queenside is tied down for a long time. The knight defends b7 and gains a move at the same time.}
) ( 7... e6? {It defends d5 and forgets b7: 8.Qxb7 simply wins a pawn.} ) ) 3... Nf6
{Develop and hold d5 for one more move. [%tags development]} 4. Nc3
{The main line. [%weight 8]} ( 4. e3
{Exercise three, the Slow Slav. Play to move ten and find out what the open h-file is worth. [%weight 6]}
4... Bf5 {Out, because nothing can punish it. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares]}
5. Nc3 {White develops. [%weight 6]} 5... e6
{Solid, behind the bishop rather than in front of it. [%tags development]} 6. Nh4
{White goes after the bishop. [%weight 5]} 6... Bg6
{Let the trade happen. [%tags bishop-placement]} 7. Nxg6
{White gives up a knight for the bishop. [%weight 8]} 7... hxg6
{Take with the h-pawn. Remember this move when you reach the end of the line. [%tags h-file]}
8. Bd3 {White develops. [%weight 6]} 8... Nbd7
{Develop towards the centre. [%tags development]} 9. O-O
{White castles short. [%weight 7]} 9... Bd6
{The bishop takes the diagonal that ends on h2. [%tags bishop-placement]} 10. Qc2
{A natural developing move. Black to play: White's king has no escape square, and Black has a rook on an open file and a bishop aimed at h2. [%weight 3]}
10... Bxh2+
{The bishop cannot be captured. The rook on h8 defends h2 down the file that ...hxg6 opened, so 11.Kxh2 is not a legal move at all. [%tags tactics h-file] [%plan After the forced 11.Kh1, play ...Bd6+ with discovered check from the rook, then bring the knight to g4 and the queen to h4.] [%line The quiet lines: the h2 pawn]}
( 10... Qe7?
{A sensible move that throws the game away. When a rook sits on an open file behind a bishop pointing at h2, look at the sacrifice before anything else.}
) ) 4... dxc4
{Take, to free the bishop and make White spend a move. [%tags development light-squares]}
5. a4 {White stops ...b5. [%weight 8]} 5... Bf5
{The bishop goes outside the pawn chain. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares]} 6. e3
{The main line, and exercise one. Play through to move fourteen and find Black's answer to the pawn on e5. [%weight 7]}
( 6. Ne5
{Exercise two. Play to move eleven and find the move that keeps everything defended. [%weight 5]}
6... Nbd7 {Challenge the knight. [%tags development]} 7. Nxc4
{White wins the pawn back. [%weight 7]} 7... Qc7 {The queen eyes e5. [%tags development]}
8. g3 {White fianchettoes. [%weight 6]} 8... e5
{The break, with White's king bishop still at home. [%tags e5-break]} 9. dxe5
{White takes. [%weight 7]} 9... Nxe5 {Recapture with the knight. [%tags e5-break]} 10. Bf4
{White develops with a hit on the knight, and pins it against the queen on c7. [%weight 7]}
10... Nfd7 {A second defender rather than a retreat. [%tags development]} 11. Bg2
{White finishes the fianchetto. Black to play: the knight on c4 is loose and taking it loses the game. Find the move that settles the centre instead. [%weight 7]}
11... f6
{Hold the knight on e5 with a pawn. Taking on c4 instead loses the queen to Bxc7, because the knight on e5 is the only thing keeping that diagonal shut. [%tags tactics e5-break] [%plan Follow with ...Be7, ...O-O and ...Rd8. The knight on e5 is a permanent asset once a pawn is holding it there.] [%line 6.Ne5: the ...e5 push]}
( 11... Nxc4?
{12.Bxc7 and the queen is gone. The knight on e5 was pinned along the f4 to c7 diagonal the whole time.}
) ( 11... Be7!?
{Develops, and it leaves the knight on e5 held by pieces alone. A pawn does the job better and for good.}
) ) 6... e6 {Safe now that the bishop is out. [%tags development]} 7. Bxc4
{White recovers the pawn. [%weight 8]} 7... Bb4
{Pin the knight and prepare to castle. [%tags development]} 8. O-O
{White castles. [%weight 8]} 8... Nbd7 {Develop the last knight. [%tags development]}
9. Qe2 {White prepares the e4 break. [%weight 7]} 9... Bg6
{Step off the square the pawn is coming to. [%tags bishop-placement]} 10. e4
{The break arrives. [%weight 7]} 10... O-O {King first. [%tags king-safety]} 11. Bd3
{White asks the bishop a question. [%weight 7]} 11... Bh5
{Dodge the trade and eye the knight on f3. [%tags bishop-placement]} 12. e5
{White pushes the pawn to e5 and takes a wedge of space. [%weight 7]} 12... Nd5
{The knight takes the best square available. [%tags development]} 13. Nxd5
{White trades. [%weight 7]} 13... cxd5
{Take with the c-pawn and open the file. [%tags c-file]} 14. Qe3
{White supports the wedge and eyes the kingside. Black to play: the pawn on e5 is cramping the whole position, so what does something about it? [%weight 6]}
14... f6
{Strike the wedge. The pawn on e5 is what makes Black's position uncomfortable, and it has to be challenged before White builds around it. [%tags e5-break c-file] [%plan After exf6 recapture with a knight or the queen and use the half-open f-file. If White leaves the tension, take on e5 yourself and the cramp is gone.] [%line The main line: the ...f6 break]}
( 14... Be7!?
{Sensible development, and it leaves the pawn on e5 exactly where White wants it. The break is the point of the position.}
) ( 14... Bxf3?!
{15.gxf3 damages White's kingside and gives up Black's best minor piece for a knight that was not doing much.}
